== 1.7 Chapter 1 Practice Drill — 40 Questions

40 questions graded in the order the CBT engine is likely to meet them. Allow 44 minutes; the key and a full solution for every question follow.

#mcq(1, [When a comprehension passage is followed by four questions, the first thing to read is],
  [the questions and their options],
  [the passage],
  [the title],
  [the first paragraph twice]
)

#mcq(2, [“Lagos has more than fifteen million people. That figure explains why its transport system is permanently at breaking point.” The topic sentence of this paragraph is],
  [both sentences equally],
  [neither, because there is no claim],
  [the second sentence],
  [the first sentence]
)

#mcq(3, [A paragraph whose first sentence is “For instance, in Kano the scheme failed” most probably has its claim],
  [in the sentence after the example],
  [in that first sentence],
  [in the last sentence only],
  [in the previous paragraph]
)

#mcq(4, [Which option would be WRONG as the answer to “What is the passage mainly about?”],
  [a claim supported by every paragraph],
  [a claim supported by one paragraph],
  [the conclusion the writer reaches],
  [the argument the examples prove]
)

#mcq(5, [“Nobody is told that the toll will cost a trader half a day's profit… A bridge that only cars can use is not a bridge for a market town.” The writer's purpose is to],
  [compare two towns],
  [explain toll collection],
  [argue that the bridge ignores the town that needs it],
  [describe a bridge design]
)

#mcq(6, [“The ministry published the rules and gave the schools six weeks to comply.” The pronoun that can replace *the schools* is],
  [them],
  [it],
  [their],
  [they]
)

#mcq(7, [An option that sums up the passage's subject but makes no claim is best described as],
  [too broad],
  [too narrow],
  [a reversal],
  [an invention]
)

#mcq(8, [“The exercise books were not the problem” is a sentence that usually],
  [ends the passage],
  [turns the argument towards its claim],
  [repeats the first sentence],
  [introduces the support]
)

#mcq(9, [Skimming a passage means reading],
  [only the questions],
  [every word quickly],
  [the longest paragraph twice],
  [the first and last sentence of each paragraph]
)

#mcq(10, [The main idea of a passage must be supported by],
  [at least one paragraph],
  [the first paragraph],
  [every paragraph],
  [the last paragraph]
)

#mcq(11, [“The council has promised a lorry park for eleven years; the file has moved between three departments and been lost once.” The main idea is that],
  [a file was lost],
  [the council has promised a lorry park],
  [the council has three departments],
  [the promise has been repeatedly shelved]
)

#mcq(12, [A candidate who answers comprehension questions from memory rather than from the sentence is most likely to],
  [run out of time],
  [lose marks for spelling],
  [choose a true statement that answers a different question],
  [misread the instructions]
)

#mcq(13, [“According to the passage” introduces a question that wants],
  [the main idea],
  [a stated fact],
  [an inference],
  [the writer's opinion]
)

#mcq(14, [The best title for a passage is],
  [a sentence stating one detail],
  [a phrase wide enough to cover every paragraph],
  [the first sentence of the passage],
  [the longest option]
)

#mcq(15, [“Which of the following is NOT stated in the passage?” The word NOT tells you that],
  [three options are false],
  [all four options are stated],
  [the passage contradicts itself],
  [three options are stated and one is not]
)

#mcq(16, [A passage that describes a problem in the first paragraph and a solution in the second has its main idea],
  [in the first paragraph],
  [in the second paragraph],
  [in neither],
  [split evenly between them]
)

#mcq(17, [The words *however*, *moreover* and *in short* are useful because they],
  [make the passage longer],
  [signpost a turn, an addition or a summary],
  [show the writer's tone],
  [are always the topic sentence]
)

#mcq(18, [When the last sentence of a passage is a judgement rather than a fact, it usually carries],
  [a quotation],
  [the supporting detail],
  [a definition],
  [the writer's purpose or verdict]
)

#mcq(19, [A paragraph that opens with a question most often puts its claim],
  [in the previous paragraph],
  [in the question],
  [nowhere],
  [in the sentence after the question]
)

#mcq(20, [“In short, the clinic needed a system, not more exercise books.” The phrase *in short* signals that the sentence],
  [adds a new example],
  [restates the claim in fewer words],
  [introduces a quotation],
  [contradicts the passage]
)

#mcq(21, [The safest way to check a chosen option is to],
  [compare it with the other options],
  [read the passage again],
  [put the option back into the sentence it came from],
  [choose the longest option]
)

#mcq(22, [A question that quotes a line number tells you],
  [that the question is a trap],
  [that the passage is difficult],
  [where to start reading],
  [where the answer is]
)

#mcq(23, [Which of these is a claim rather than a topic?],
  [Kano, its roads and its people],
  [Roads in Kano are repaired only in election years],
  [The state of the roads in Kano],
  [A description of Kano's roads]
)

#mcq(24, [The single most expensive habit in a comprehension paper is],
  [checking the answer],
  [reading the options],
  [reading the passage before the questions],
  [marking the passage]
)

#mcq(25, [“The scheme was praised by the governor, whose officials had designed it.” A candidate who needs to know who designed the scheme should notice],
  [the passive verb],
  [the relative clause *whose officials had designed it*],
  [the tense of *praised*],
  [the word *scheme*]
)

#mcq(26, [A detail may be used as evidence in a main-idea answer only if],
  [it is the first sentence],
  [it supports the claim you are choosing],
  [it is repeated twice],
  [it contains a number]
)

#mcq(27, [“The traders moved two hundred metres and set the stones down again.” In a passage arguing that the traders govern themselves, this sentence functions as],
  [a counter-argument],
  [the conclusion],
  [the claim],
  [evidence for the claim]
)

#mcq(28, [Reading the four questions first is most useful when],
  [the questions name people, lines or quoted phrases],
  [the passage is short],
  [the options are short],
  [the passage is long]
)

#mcq(29, [The main idea of a two-sentence passage is usually found by],
  [joining what both sentences claim],
  [looking for a quotation],
  [taking the first sentence],
  [taking the second sentence]
)

#mcq(30, [“He has refused to sanction the plan.” If *sanction* here means *approve*, the sentence says the man has],
  [announced it],
  [funded it],
  [declined to approve it],
  [punished the plan]
)

#mcq(31, [The purpose of numbering paragraphs in the margin is],
  [to let a question send you straight to a paragraph],
  [to find spelling errors],
  [to show the examiner you read it],
  [to count the words]
)

#mcq(32, [“Which of the following best summarises the passage?” differs from “According to the passage…” because the first asks for],
  [a definition],
  [a quotation],
  [a claim covering the whole passage],
  [a detail]
)

#mcq(33, [An option that reverses the cause and effect of the passage is],
  [almost certainly the key],
  [a trap built from a true sentence],
  [an inference],
  [a restatement]
)

#mcq(34, [When two options both seem possible, the one to prefer is the one],
  [whose supporting sentence you can point at],
  [that repeats the passage's words],
  [that is longer],
  [that comes first]
)

#mcq(35, [A passage about a failed scheme that ends “the lesson was learned by nobody” is most likely],
  [critical],
  [neutral],
  [celebratory],
  [approving]
)

#mcq(36, [The fastest way to find the sentence that answers a detail question is to],
  [look at the options],
  [scan for the question's key word],
  [read the passage again],
  [read every first sentence]
)

#mcq(37, [A candidate who has spent five minutes on a passage has time left for],
  [a second reading],
  [nothing],
  [another passage],
  [about a minute of checking and answering]
)

#mcq(38, [“The file has moved between three departments and been lost once.” The tone of this sentence is],
  [deadpan],
  [apologetic],
  [admiring],
  [alarmed]
)

#mcq(39, [Which question is answered by the passage as a whole rather than by one sentence?],
  [What is the population of Lagos?],
  [Who lost the file?],
  [Why does the market road fail?],
  [When was the lorry park promised?]
)

#mcq(40, [The final minute of a comprehension section is best spent],
  [reading the instructions again],
  [re-reading the passage],
  [changing doubtful answers],
  [checking that every question has an answer]
)

#shortcut-box("Before you turn to the key")[
  + Mark the whole set in *one pass* — every answer, right or wrong — before you read a single solution.
  + Write the score at the top of the page, because a drill you cannot compare with the last one tells you nothing.
  + Then read *every* solution, including the ones you got right: the second method is in the solution, not in the chapter.
]

